# ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Production environment — loaded automatically by Vite for `npm run build`.
# Non-secret values only; this file is committed. Real keys go in the deploy
# environment or env/.env.production.local (git-ignored).
#
# Wi-Mall production hosts:
#   wi-mall.com          main website (owns login)
#   agency.wi-mall.com   this app (Wi-Agency)
#   agent.wi-mall.com    agent web
#   vendor.wi-mall.com   vendor dashboard
#   api.wi-mall.com      backend API
# ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# wi-mall backend — all /api calls go here. Must end in /api.
VITE_API_BASE_URL=https://api.wi-mall.com/api

# Base URL where uploaded files are served (defaults to the API origin /uploads).
# VITE_FILE_BASE_URL=

# App display name.
VITE_APP_NAME=Wi-Agency

# Where `/login` hands off — the main site owns the login screen.
VITE_LOGIN_URL=https://wi-mall.com/login

# This app on the web. Only the NATIVE build reads it: billing is read-only on
# mobile (D4 / Phase 5), so plan changes and credit top-ups point here. Same
# value as the code default; stated anyway, because a silent default is the kind
# of thing that survives a domain change.
VITE_WEB_DASHBOARD_URL=https://agency.wi-mall.com

# ─── Live tracking (geo-tracker, the separate Go WebSocket service) ────────────
# RESOLVED 2026-09-13 — geo-tracker is deployed and this is its real host.
# Verified live on that date: GET https://track.wi-mall.com/readyz answers 200,
# and a CORS preflight carrying Origin: https://agency.wi-mall.com comes back with
# a matching Access-Control-Allow-Origin, so this origin is in geo-tracker's
# ALLOWED_ORIGINS — which is the same setting that governs its WebSocket origin
# check, not a separate one.
#
# ⚠ ONE VARIABLE DOES BOTH SIDES. The HTTP base is DERIVED from this URL's origin
#   (wss -> https) in src/services/geo-tracker.service.ts, so VITE_GEO_TRACKER_URL
#   stays unset deliberately. Set it only if the socket and the HTTP reads are ever
#   genuinely split across two origins — setting it to the same host would simply
#   be a second place for the hostname to go stale.
VITE_GEO_TRACKER_WS_URL=wss://track.wi-mall.com/ws/track
# VITE_GEO_TRACKER_URL=

# Bearer-token override for the geo-tracker WS. Only needed when geo-tracker is
# cross-site from agency.wi-mall.com — a SameSite=Lax access_token cookie is not
# sent on a cross-site handshake. Prefer injecting window.wiMallGetAccessToken()
# over baking a JWT into the bundle.
#
# ✅ UNSET IS CORRECT, AND THE BACKEND SETTING IT RELIES ON IS CONFIRMED PRESENT.
#   agency.wi-mall.com and track.wi-mall.com are SAME-SITE (one registrable
#   domain, wi-mall.com), so a Lax cookie is sent on the handshake and no token is
#   needed — provided the cookie is scoped to the parent domain. jovi-mall sets its
#   auth cookies with domain = AUTH_COOKIE_DOMAIN in production
#   (src/config/cookie.config.ts), and the deployed environment has
#   AUTH_COOKIE_DOMAIN=.wi-mall.com. Checked 2026-09-13.
#
#   ⚠ KEPT BECAUSE IT IS STILL THE FIRST THING TO CHECK IF TRACKING EVER BREAKS.
#   Were that variable ever emptied, the cookie becomes host-only to
#   api.wi-mall.com and never reaches track.wi-mall.com — and the symptom misleads:
#   every ordinary API call keeps working, because those go to the host that owns
#   the cookie, and ONLY Live Tracking fails, as a socket that opens and is then
#   closed unauthenticated. It looks like a geo-tracker fault rather than a
#   cookie-scope one.
#
#   ⚠ The fix would be on jovi-mall, never a token here. Baking a JWT into a public
#   bundle would hand every visitor a live credential.

VITE_GEO_TRACKER_TOKEN=

# ─── Live Tracking basemap (Leaflet) ──────────────────────────────────────────
# ⚠ THE COMMENT THAT USED TO BE HERE WAS WRONG ABOUT THE DEFAULT. It said unset
#   falls back to "CARTO, keyless". src/config/map.ts sets
#   DEFAULT_PROVIDER = OSM, and its CARTO provider is exported as
#   cartoWithKey(apiKey) — a function, specifically so it cannot be used without a
#   key. Nothing wires CARTO today. Corrected 2026-09-13 by reading the source.
#
# So unset means plain OpenStreetMap, which DOES render, unwatermarked, with no
# account — and that is the one thing worth knowing here: the map will look
# completely fine on launch day, so nothing will prompt anyone to revisit it.
#
# ⚠ OPEN ITEM, NOT A BLOCKER. OSM's Tile Usage Policy asks commercial services not
#   to use their tile servers, and this is a commercial delivery dashboard. The
#   map.ts comment says the same thing: OSM is "the right default and the wrong
#   thing to leave pointed at a production dashboard under real traffic". Provision
#   a provider with a plan (CARTO, MapTiler, Stadia) and set the three variables
#   below; no code change is needed, which is why this is configuration.
#
# ⚠ Whatever key you use must be DOMAIN-RESTRICTED. These values are inlined into
#   a public bundle.
# VITE_MAP_TILE_URL=
# VITE_MAP_TILE_URL_DARK=
# VITE_MAP_TILE_ATTRIBUTION=

# ─── Payments ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# RESOLVED 2026-09-13 — LEAVE THIS UNSET. It is no longer waiting on infra input.
#
# Stripe is switched OFF on the backend, deliberately: STRIPE_SECRET_KEY is absent
# from the deployed jovi-mall environment, and its absence IS the off switch
# (backend/docs/DEPLOY-DOKPLOY.md, "What is not covered here"). A publishable key
# is only ever half of a pair — setting one here would surface a card option in the
# billing UI whose other half does not exist, so the upgrade would fail after the
# agency had already chosen it.
#
# Unset is therefore the correct production value rather than a gap: the billing UI
# offers mobile money only, which is what the platform can actually take. NotchPay
# and MyCoolPay carry real payments.
#
# When Stripe is genuinely switched on, the backend secret key goes in FIRST and the
# pk_live_… value here second — in that order, so there is never a window where the
# UI offers what the server cannot complete.
# VITE_STRIPE_PUBLISHABLE_KEY=

# ─── Push notifications (FCM web push) ────────────────────────────────────────
# The messaging Firebase project is `bingoo-22222`, and it IS the production one
# — the same project backs dev and production, by the project owner's decision
# (2026-09-09). These are its WEB app registration; android/app/google-services.json
# holds the ANDROID registration of the same project, which is why its apiKey and
# appId differ from these and are supposed to.
#
# Publishable by design: a Firebase web config is shipped to every browser that
# loads the app. What actually guards a topic is the server key, which is not here.
#
# ⚠ KEEP IN SYNC with the hardcoded copy in public/firebase-messaging-sw.js — a
# service worker cannot read Vite env vars, so there are two copies and nothing
# checks them against each other. Drift is silent: web push registers and then
# never delivers.
#
# When ANY of these is unset the provider is not installed at all, the settings
# screen says push is unconfigured, and in-app notifications carry on working.
VITE_FIREBASE_API_KEY=AIzaSyBEm8btwePIHHYpSs41zebr6KmHIuWinA0
VITE_FIREBASE_AUTH_DOMAIN=bingoo-22222.firebaseapp.com
VITE_FIREBASE_PROJECT_ID=bingoo-22222
VITE_FIREBASE_MESSAGING_SENDER_ID=741831724264
VITE_FIREBASE_APP_ID=1:741831724264:web:6741494f37bd0bd0d34d5d
VITE_FIREBASE_VAPID_KEY=BCnGJqU4hsphcmSNMlMUuwcKIDbvKXIU8lF85ylFCkIgrLbBOE9G6-Idq5xVhiFWbY_DdGmLUZSc309gBZo5-gA
